== Motivation ==

Fidelity bonds are used to resist sybil attacks in certain decentralized anonymous protocols. They are created by locking up bitcoins using the `OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY` opcode.

Having a common derivation scheme allows users of wallet software to have a backup of their fidelity bonds by storing only the HD seed and a reference to this BIP. Importantly the user does not need to backup any timelock values.

We largely use the same account-structure approach used in BIPs 44, 49, 84 and 86 for ease of implementation.

This allows keeping the private keys of fidelity bonds in cold storage, which increases the sybil resistance of a system without hot wallet risk.

== Backwards Compatibility ==

This BIP is not backwards compatible with generic wallets by design, as described in the Considerations section of [[bip-0049.mediawiki|BIP 49]]. A wallet that does not implement this BIP at all will not discover fidelity bonds and the user will notice that something is wrong.

Within this BIP, Version 1 and Version 2 use different <tt>purpose</tt> fields and different <tt>witness script</tt> templates (see [[#public-key-derivation|Public key derivation]] and [[#address-derivation|Address derivation]]), so they occupy disjoint parts of the derivation tree and neither collides with nor overwrites the other. This lets an implementation support both at once: Version 1 is kept in the spec, unchanged from the deployed JoinMarket scheme, purely so that software can still find and spend fidelity bonds created before Version 2 existed. Version 2 is the scheme new fidelity bonds should be created under, since it gives fidelity bonds their own <tt>purpose</tt> and uses a <tt>witness script</tt> that Miniscript-aware signing devices can parse.

A wallet implementing only Version 1 will not discover Version 2 fidelity bonds, and a wallet implementing only Version 2 will not discover Version 1 fidelity bonds created by earlier software. Wallets SHOULD implement both: generate and check Version 2 addresses for new fidelity bonds, and additionally scan Version 1 addresses so that funds already locked up under the original scheme remain discoverable until they are spent or expire.

== Background ==

=== Fidelity bonds ===

A fidelity bond is a mechanism where bitcoin value is deliberately sacrificed to make a cryptographic identity expensive to obtain. A way to create a fidelity bond is to lock up bitcoins by sending them to a timelocked address. The valuable thing being sacrificed is the time-value-of-money.

The sacrifice must be done in a way that can be proven to a third party. This proof can be made by showing the UTXO outpoint, the address redeemscript and a signature which signs a message using the private key corresponding to the public key in the redeemscript.

The sacrificed value is an objective measurement that can't be faked and which can be verified by anybody (just like, for example PoW mining). Sybil attacks can be made very expensive by forcing a hypothetical sybil attacker to lock up many bitcoins for a long time. JoinMarket implements fidelity bonds for protection from sybil attackers. At the time of writing over 600 BTC in total have been locked up with some for many years. Their UTXOs and signatures have been advertised to the world as proof. We can calculate that for a sybil attacker to succeed in unmixing all the CoinJoins, they would have to lock up over 100k BTC for several years.

=== Fidelity bonds in cold storage ===

To allow for holding fidelity bonds in cold storage, there is an intermediate keypair called the certificate.

    UTXO key ---signs---> certificate ---signs---> endpoint

Where the endpoint might be a IRC nickname or Tor onion hostname. The certificate keypair can be kept online and used to prove ownership of the fidelity bond. Even if the hot wallet private keys are stolen, the coins in the timelocked address will still be safe, although the thief will be able to impersonate the fidelity bond until the expiry.

== Rationale ==

It is useful for the user to avoid having to keep a record of the timelocks in the time-locked addresses. So only a limited small set of timelocks are defined by this BIP. This way the user must only store their seed phrase, and knowledge that they have coins stored using this BIP standard. The user doesn't need to remember or store any dates.

Version 1 of this standard is already implemented and deployed in JoinMarket, using derivation path <tt>m/84'/0'/0'/2/index</tt> and the <tt>witness script</tt> `<timelock> OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY OP_DROP <derived_key> OP_CHECKSIG`. As it is a protocol used by a live system, Version 1 itself is kept as-is and is more about documenting something which already exists, warts and all, rather than a target for further changes.

Subsequent review identified two aspects of Version 1 worth revising for newly-created fidelity bonds, which this BIP addresses by defining Version 2 as an additional scheme alongside Version 1 rather than by changing Version 1 in place:

Version 1 derives addresses under <tt>m/84'/0'/0'/2/index</tt>, reusing BIP 84's <tt>purpose</tt> field the way BIP 49 layers P2WPKH-in-P2SH accounts under BIP 44's <tt>purpose</tt>. Unlike BIP 49's case, there is no structural reason for fidelity bonds to share BIP 84's purpose: fidelity bond addresses are not P2WPKH outputs and are not interchangeable with plain BIP 84 accounts, so reusing <tt>84'</tt> only obscured which BIP a given path belongs to. Per BIP 43, each application-specific derivation scheme should register its own <tt>purpose</tt>, so Version 2 gives fidelity bonds their own <tt>purpose</tt> of <tt>46'</tt>, matching this BIP's number as BIP 44/49/84/86 do for theirs. A dedicated purpose field also makes it simpler for hardware wallets and other signing devices to recognize and offer explicit support for fidelity bond derivation, rather than having to special-case index ranges within their existing BIP 84 handling.

Version 1's <tt>witness script</tt> cannot be expressed in [[bip-0379.md|Miniscript]] because Miniscript requires every script fragment to leave a clean boolean on the stack, and the <tt>OP_DROP</tt> in the middle of this template breaks that property. This meant Miniscript-aware signing devices could not recognize, display or sign for fidelity bond outputs without bespoke, non-Miniscript support. Version 2 reorders the template to `<derived_key> OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY <timelock> OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY`, which is exactly the Miniscript fragment <tt>and_v(v:pk(K),after(T))</tt> and is functionally equivalent to Version 1's script (same signature and timelock check, same one-branch spending condition). A prior proposal to shrink the script by one byte was rejected as not worth a compatibility break on its own; the Miniscript compatibility benefit is the actual justification for Version 2's script template.

Version 1 is retained rather than replaced because existing fidelity bonds were created under it, are already advertised as proof of sacrifice, and may still be timelocked years into the future; a wallet that dropped Version 1 support outright would simply stop finding those funds. Defining Version 2 as an additional scheme lets new bonds get the benefits above without making existing bonds unrecoverable.

An open question raised during review is whether the certificate message should additionally bind the delegation to the specific bond script and network, e.g. `fidelity-bond-cert-v2|<bond address>|<cert_pubkey>|<cert_expiry>`, which would keep the message pure ASCII and human-signable while preventing a certificate from being replayed against a different script or network that happens to share the same key. This BIP does not adopt such a binding: fidelity bonds are meant to represent a single objective, unambiguous sacrifice, and letting one key back certificates for multiple bonds or networks would make that sacrifice harder to measure. A future revision may want to revisit this if a concrete need for it materializes.

== Specifications ==

This BIP defines the two needed steps to derive multiple deterministic addresses based on a [[bip-0032.mediawiki|BIP 32]] master private key. It also defines the format of the certificate that can be signed by the deterministic address key.

=== Public key derivation ===

To derive a public key from the root account, this BIP uses a similar account-structure as defined in BIP [[bip-0044.mediawiki|44]]. The <tt>purpose</tt> field and the <tt>change</tt> field both depend on the version:

Version 1 (legacy):
<pre>
m / 84' / 0' / 0' / 2 / index
</pre>

Version 2:
<pre>
m / 46' / 0' / 0' / 0 / index
</pre>

Version 1 reuses BIP 84's <tt>purpose</tt> field; it is retained unchanged from the deployed JoinMarket scheme purely so existing fidelity bonds remain discoverable, see [[#rationale|Rationale]]. Because Version 1's <tt>account</tt> level (<tt>m/84'/0'/0'</tt>) is shared with a wallet's ordinary BIP 84 receive and change addresses, Version 1 sets <tt>change</tt> to <tt>2</tt>, a value BIP 44 never uses for its external (<tt>0</tt>) or internal (<tt>1</tt>) chains, so that fidelity bond addresses cannot collide with them.

Version 2 uses its own <tt>purpose</tt> field of <tt>46'</tt>, matching this BIP's number as recommended by [[bip-0043.mediawiki|BIP 43]], and SHOULD be used for creating new fidelity bonds. Nothing else is derived under <tt>m/46'</tt>, so there is no shared account to collide with and no need for the <tt>change</tt> workaround Version 1 relies on; Version 2 uses the conventional external-chain value of <tt>0</tt> instead. This also makes Version 2 paths look like an ordinary BIP 44-style path to generic path-sanity checks in signing devices, rather than tripping "unusual path" warnings the way Version 1's <tt>2</tt> can.

A key derived with either of these derivation path patterns will be referred to as <tt>derived_key</tt> further in this document; which version's path was used to derive it should be tracked alongside it, since it also determines which <tt>witness script</tt> template applies (see [[#address-derivation|Address derivation]]).

For <tt>index</tt>, addresses are numbered from 0 in a sequentially increasing manner with a fixed upper bound: The index only goes up to <tt>959</tt> inclusive. Only 960 addresses can be derived per version for a given BIP32 master key. Furthermore there is no concept of a gap limit, instead wallets must always generate all 960 addresses of a version and check for all of them if they have a balance and history.

=== Timelock derivation ===

The timelock used in the time-locked address is derived from the <tt>index</tt>. The timelock is a unix time. It is always at the start of the first second at the beginning of the month (see [[#test-vectors|Test vectors]]). The <tt>index</tt> counts upwards the months from January 2020, ending in December 2099. At 12 months per year for 80 years this totals 960 timelocks. Note that care must be taken with the year 2038 problem on 32-bit systems.

<pre>
year = 2020 + index // 12
month = 1 + index % 12
</pre>


=== Address derivation ===

To derive the address from the above calculated public key and timelock, we create a <tt>witness script</tt> which locks the funds until the <tt>timelock</tt> and checks the signature of the <tt>derived_key</tt>. The <tt>witness script</tt> is hashed with SHA256 to produce a 32-byte hash value that forms the <tt>witness program</tt> in the output script of the P2WSH address. The template used depends on the version of the <tt>derived_key</tt>:

Version 1 (legacy):
    witnessScript: <timelock> OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY OP_DROP <derived_key> OP_CHECKSIG

Version 2:
    witnessScript: <derived_key> OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY <timelock> OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY

For both versions:
    witness:      <signature> <witnessScript>
    scriptSig:    (empty)
    scriptPubKey: 0 <32-byte-hash>
                  (0x0020{32-byte-hash})

The Version 2 template is written <tt>and_v(v:pk(K),after(T))</tt> in [[bip-0379.md|Miniscript]] notation, where <tt>K</tt> is <tt>derived_key</tt> and <tt>T</tt> is <tt>timelock</tt>. Unlike the Version 1 template, it is directly expressible in Miniscript, letting Miniscript-aware signing devices recognize, display and sign for Version 2 fidelity bond outputs without bespoke policy support. The Version 1 template is not expressible in Miniscript; see [[#rationale|Rationale]].

Generic Miniscript support does not by itself make a signer capable of recognizing every Version 2 fidelity bond out of the box, however. A [[bip-0388.mediawiki|BIP 388]] wallet policy describes one output descriptor with ranged keys, but a fidelity bond account also varies its timelock per index (see [[#timelock-derivation|Timelock derivation]]): <tt>K</tt> is a plain ranged key derivable from the account xpub, but <tt>T</tt> is not expressible as a BIP 388 range, since it follows this BIP's index-to-month mapping rather than a linear or xpub-derived sequence. In practice a single fixed <tt>after(T)</tt> policy describes one specific bond, not a whole account of 960 of them, so a signer needs bespoke support for this BIP's timelock schedule in addition to generic Miniscript parsing to fully support Version 2 fidelity bonds.

=== Message signing ===

In order to support signing of certificates, implementers should support signing ASCII messages.

The certificate message is defined as `"fidelity-bond-cert" || "|" || cert_pubkey || "|" || cert_expiry`, where `cert_pubkey` is the 33-byte compressed certificate public key encoded as exactly 66 lowercase hexadecimal characters, and `cert_expiry` is encoded as its shortest decimal representation (no leading zeros). The reference JoinMarket implementation historically signs the raw 33 bytes of `cert_pubkey` rather than its hex encoding, and existing JoinMarket software accepts both forms; this BIP requires the ASCII hex form for Version 2 so that the whole certificate message stays pure ASCII, which matters for hardware wallets and other signers that can only display and sign ASCII text.

The certificate expiry `cert_expiry` is the number of the 2016-block period after which the certificate is no longer valid. For example, if `cert_expiry` is 330 then the certificate will become invalid after block height 665280 (:=330x2016). The purpose of the expiry parameter is so that in case the certificate keypair is compromised, the attacker can only impersonate the fidelity bond for a limited amount of time.

A certificate message can be created by another application external to this standard. It is then signed the same way as Bitcoin's widely-implemented "Sign Message" function: form the preimage `CompactSize(24) || "Bitcoin Signed Message:\n" || CompactSize(len(message)) || message`, where `CompactSize` is Bitcoin's variable-length integer encoding and `message` is the ASCII message bytes (the fixed 24-byte magic string always has the single-byte `CompactSize` prefix `0x18`); hash the preimage with SHA256d; and sign the resulting digest with ECDSA over secp256k1 using a low-S, 65-byte compact recoverable signature (1-byte header `27 + recovery_id + 4` for a compressed public key, followed by the 32-byte `r` and 32-byte `s` values), base64-encoded. The signature is made with the private key of the <tt>derived_key</tt>. Some existing tooling, such as JoinMarket's fidelity bond proof format, instead stores this signature DER-encoded together with a separate recovery flag inside a fixed 252-byte structure; implementers interoperating with that format need to convert between the two encodings.

Almost all wallets implementing this standard can use their already-existing "Sign Message" function to sign the certificate message. As the certificate message itself is always an ASCII string, the wallet may not need to specially implement this section at all but just rely on users copypasting their certificate message into the already-existing "Sign Message" user interface. This works as long as the wallet knows how to use the private key of the timelocked address for signing messages.

It is most important for wallet implementations of this standard to support creating the certificate signature. Verifying the certificate signature is less important.


